- list_calendars(): Every calendar, with its account and whether it takes new events.
- list_events(account?, calendar?, from?, limit?, search?, to?): Events between two days, earliest first: today's when no days are given, and one day's with only from. A date is today, tomorrow, yesterday, 2026-10-06, or 2026-10-06T14:30 in local time (a Z or an offset also works).
- add_event(account?, calendar?, end?, location?, notes?, start, title): Add an event. It lasts an hour unless end says otherwise, and goes in the default calendar unless calendar names another. A date is today, tomorrow, yesterday, 2026-10-06, or 2026-10-06T14:30 in local time (a Z or an offset also works).

The first time a chat uses Calendar, macOS may ask the person. Exit code 2 means macOS keeps Instrument out of Calendar: the person now sees a card that lets them change it, and their answer wakes you, so stop there and say what you're waiting for.",
    "Contacts on this Mac, with every account added to it.

\`instrument app call apple-contacts <tool> '<json>'\` runs a tool, and \`instrument app tool apple-contacts <tool>\` shows the JSON it takes.

- search_contacts(limit?, search?): People whose name, nickname, company, title, email or phone holds every word of search, with their emails, phones and birthday; everyone without search.

The first time a chat uses Contacts, macOS may ask the person. Exit code 2 means macOS keeps Instrument out of Contacts: the person now sees a card that lets them change it, and their answer wakes you, so stop there and say what you're waiting for.",
    "Mail on this Mac, with every account added to it, Gmail and Exchange ones included. It can't flag a message or save its attachments, and a reply goes out as a new message rather than in its thread.

\`instrument app call apple-mail <tool> '<json>'\` runs a tool, and \`instrument app tool apple-mail <tool>\` shows the JSON it takes.

- list_accounts(): Every account added to Mail, with its addresses and whether it is on.
- list_mailboxes(account?): The mailboxes of every account that is on, or of one, with how many messages in each are unread.
- list_messages(account?, limit?, mailbox?, unread?): The newest messages: from every account's inbox, from one account's with account, or from the mailbox named, which goes with account.
- search_messages(account?, limit?, mailbox?, query): Messages whose subject or sender holds the words, as one phrase, newest first: from every mailbox unless account or mailbox narrows it, and the more mailboxes, the longer it takes.
- read_message(account?, id, mailbox?): One message whole: who it is from and to, when it came, the names of its attachments, and its text. Passing the account and mailbox list_messages or search_messages printed finds it faster.
- draft_message(attachments?, bcc?, body?, cc?, from?, subject?, to?): Open a new message in Mail with all of this filled in, for the person to look over and send themselves.
- send_message(attachments?, bcc?, body?, cc?, from?, subject?, to): Send a message now. Send only what the person asked you to send, worded as they saw it.
- move_message(account?, id, mailbox?, to_mailbox): Move a message to another mailbox of its own account, such as Archive or Trash. account and mailbox say where it is now, as list_messages printed them.

The first time a chat uses Mail, macOS may ask the person. Exit code 2 means macOS keeps Instrument out of Mail: the person now sees a card that lets them change it, and their answer wakes you, so stop there and say what you're waiting for.",
    "Messages on this Mac, which sends iMessages, and texts through the person's iPhone. Messages' scripting can't read conversations, so nothing here reads them; the only ways to are Full Disk Access or Accessibility for Instrument, which you never reach for on your own.

\`instrument app call apple-messages <tool> '<json>'\` runs a tool, and \`instrument app tool apple-messages <tool>\` shows the JSON it takes.

- send_message(sms?, text, to): Send a message to one person, as an iMessage, or as a text with sms, which needs their iPhone to forward texts to this Mac. Send only what the person asked you to send, worded as they saw it.

The first time a chat uses Messages, macOS may ask the person. Exit code 2 means macOS keeps Instrument out of Messages: the person now sees a card that lets them change it, and their answer wakes you, so stop there and say what you're waiting for.",
    "Notes on this Mac, with the notes synced to it, iCloud's included. A locked note can be listed but not read or changed, and a note with attachments can't be added to.

\`instrument app call apple-notes <tool> '<json>'\` runs a tool, and \`instrument app tool apple-notes <tool>\` shows the JSON it takes.

- list_folders(account?): Every folder, as a path from its account (Work/Clients), with how many notes it has.
- list_notes(account?, folder?, limit?): The notes changed most recently, from every folder or from one.
- search_notes(account?, folder?, limit?, query): Notes whose title or text holds the words, as one phrase.
- read_note(id): One note's text, by the id list_notes or search_notes printed.
- create_note(account?, body?, folder?, title?): Make a new note. Without a title, its first line is its title, as in Notes. Without a folder it goes where Notes puts new notes. Each line of body becomes a line of the note.
- append_to_note(id, text): Add lines to the end of a note, each line of text a line of the note.

The first time a chat uses Notes, macOS may ask the person. Exit code 2 means macOS keeps Instrument out of Notes: the person now sees a card that lets them change it, and their answer wakes you, so stop there and say what you're waiting for.",
    "Reminders on this Mac, with every list synced to it, iCloud's included.

\`instrument app call apple-reminders <tool> '<json>'\` runs a tool, and \`instrument app tool apple-reminders <tool>\` shows the JSON it takes.

- list_lists(): Every reminder list, with its account and whether it takes new reminders.
- list_reminders(account?, all?, due_after?, due_before?, limit?, list?, search?): Reminders not yet done, or every one with all. A date is today, tomorrow, yesterday, 2026-10-06, or 2026-10-06T14:30 in local time (a Z or an offset also works).
- add_reminder(account?, due?, list?, notes?, title): Add a reminder, to the default list unless list names another. A date is today, tomorrow, yesterday, 2026-10-06, or 2026-10-06T14:30 in local time (a Z or an offset also works).

The first time a chat uses Reminders, macOS may ask the person. Exit code 2 means macOS keeps Instrument out of Reminders: the person now sees a card that lets them change it, and their answer wakes you, so stop there and say what you're waiting for.",
  ],
  "packageScope": "The sandbox refused Node's look for a package.json above the script (/Users/mac-user/package.json). \`node <script>\` from this shell reads past that; for a script another program starts, a package.json beside the script (\`{}\` is enough) ends the look there.",
  "pageReturned": "
<instrument-system-note>
The user gave tab tab_1 back with Continue; it is now at https://example.com ("Sign in"). Look at the page before going on, since they may have changed it, then carry on with what you were doing.
</instrument-system-note>",
  "pageTaken": "
<instrument-system-note>
The user took over tab tab_1, at https://example.com, and that stopped your work. They may change the page while they have it.
</instrument-system-note>",
  "platformGuidance": [
    "The Mac's own Calendar, Contacts, Mail, Messages, Notes, Reminders are apps like any other (\`apple-calendar\`, \`apple-contacts\`, \`apple-mail\`, \`apple-messages\`, \`apple-notes\`, \`apple-reminders\`), set up from \`instrument app catalog\`.",
    undefined,
    "The shell spells a Windows path the POSIX way (\`C:\\Users\\sam\` is \`/c/Users/sam\`); when you name a path to the user, write it the Windows way. Paths you pass a program as arguments are converted, but paths written inside a script's own code are not: write those the Windows way. \`/tmp\` in the shell is the temp folder (\`%TEMP%\`); a script gets it from its language (\`tempfile.gettempdir()\`, \`os.tmpdir()\`, \`$env:TEMP\`) rather than writing \`/tmp\`. \`tasklist\` lists every process on the computer; a program you start (PowerShell's \`Get-Process\` included) may see only its own.",
    undefined,
  ],
  "runningRouterModel": "Model: you run on Mock Model from Test Provider, the model the user picked for this chat. Asked what model you are, name it plainly; you are still Instrument.",
  "signInProgress": "connected: Notion (notion); skipped by the user: Figma (figma); did not connect: Slack (slack); not answered yet: Linear (linear).",
